<article class="reading">
  <h1>How I Think</h1>

  <p>Good design makes complex systems feel simple without taking control away from the people using them. I've believed this across dev tools, healthcare, customer service software, and most recently agentic AI. Places where the work is complicated and mistakes are costly.</p>

  <p>I've been designing and building software for 25 years, and I can take an idea from sketch to shipped code. I spent years leading design teams, and these days I'm an IC by choice. I want influence, not headcount.</p>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="design-heading">
    <h2 id="design-heading">How I design now</h2>
    <p>AI does the volume and I do the judgment. Here's roughly how a project moves from a fuzzy problem to shipped code.</p>

    {% include "components/think-process.njk" %}

    <p>I start with the team, not a prompt. We work through user flows, storyboards, and whiteboards until we agree on the problem. Then AI does the heavy lifting: research, synthesizing data, and a few rough concepts.</p>

    <p>I judge those concepts, add a few of my own, and then AI and I prototype together in code, in a real browser, with real data. Figma comes in near the end for documentation and refinement. After that I stay with engineers and QA through implementation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="beliefs-heading">
    <h2 id="beliefs-heading">What I believe</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Design is decision-making.</strong> The ability to clarify tradeoffs, prioritize, and rally a team around an idea.</li>
      <li><strong>Slow down to go fast.</strong> I start low fidelity: flows, storyboards, a whiteboard. AI makes it easy to build something polished on day one, which also makes it easy to move <a href="https://www.seangood.win/articles/high-fidelity-low-definition">10x as fast in the wrong direction</a>, as Sean Good puts it. Unless the change is small, I'd rather get the problem right first and fill in the details later.</li>
      <li><strong>Rough and directionally correct beats beautiful and wrong.</strong> A good prototype shortens debates and gets real feedback fast. With AI I can get 6 to 9 rough directions on screen in a morning. The hard part is still deciding which one is good, which ones are noise, and where AI isn't the right tool at all.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence beats opinions.</strong> I'd rather watch one customer struggle with a flow than read ten opinions about what they'd probably want. I write the script and run the sessions myself, and I invite PMs and engineers to watch.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/blog/product-design-process/">Process is a tool, not a religion</a>.</strong> The team and the problem define the process, not the other way around.</li>
      <li><strong>Know which doors are one-way.</strong> Most decisions are cheap to undo, so I let them go or test them. A few are expensive to undo, and those are worth slowing down for. I'm fine being wrong. Testing on our omnichannel work proved me wrong, and the product was better for it.</li>
    </ul>

    {% include "components/think-doors.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="trust-heading">
    <h2 id="trust-heading">Designing AI people can trust</h2>
    <p>People ask me what AI should automate and what it shouldn't. My answer depends on what a mistake costs.</p>

    {% include "components/think-spectrum.njk" %}

    <p>Most of the interesting design work happens in the middle column. That's where traces, showing the evidence, and an easy way to undo do the most good. When people can see why the AI did something and roll it back, they trust it with more.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="teams-heading">
    <h2 id="teams-heading">How I work across teams</h2>
    <p>The most valuable design work happens before anything looks like a project. At Dialpad, I inherited a feature factory: siloed PMs, designers executing tickets, and engineers boxed in by technical limitations. We reorganized roadmaps around directional themes instead of disconnected deliverables. We want to ship great products, not our org chart.</p>

    <p>I also partner with customer-facing teams like support, professional services, and sales. Having those relationships beats reacting to escalations. When a customer asks for something specific, I look for the spirit of the ask and design it for everyone.</p>

    <p>Once work is underway:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>I partner with engineers early. We agree on what's safe to build before the design is finished, so engineering and design run in parallel instead of waiting on handoffs.</li>
      <li>I share work early, even when "it's not ready yet!" Engineering, PMs, QA, and leadership all get a look. The best ideas don't always come from people with design in their title.</li>
      <li>When someone asks for the unreasonable, I don't lead with no. I say "yes, and here's what it costs," lay out what we'd delay or give up, and let people decide with full information.</li>
      <li>I teach people to build. I've taught non-technical designers GitHub, set up a prototyping sandbox, and got nearly the whole design team using AI tools within six months.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="lead-heading">
    <h2 id="lead-heading">How I lead</h2>
    <p>When I first became a manager, I hired good people and stayed out of their way. Some thrived and some stalled, so I got more deliberate: notice what each person needs, coach them on it directly, and adjust as they grow.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Clear, actionable feedback.</strong> I don't sugarcoat critique or avoid hard conversations. The kindest thing a manager can do is be clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Designers should think like product people, not production artists.</strong> Craft gets you to senior. Storytelling, business sense, and selling your ideas get you further. I spent two years helping a senior designer make that shift before promoting them to staff.</li>
      <li><strong>Still in the work.</strong> I design and build alongside the team, in code against real data, with Figma for specs. Staying close to the tools keeps the coaching honest.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The goal is a team that does great work without depending on me.</p>
  </section>

  <p>Good design earns trust, from users, from teams, from the business. That's the work I care about.</p>
</article>
